fy.app\/mWOUhLM6\/raw","type":"PASTE","created_at":"2026-05-07 11:43:44.623"},{"exists":true,"id":"WNIPE6Vc","content":"-- This file was generated by galactic dumper | V2.1\n\nlocal fenv = getfenv()\nfenv['getfenv'] = function(arg1, arg2, arg3) --[[ No debug info available ]] end\nprint(\"main code running \")","title":"","encrypted":false,"user_id":"jEzo2SQk","visibility":"PUBLIC","raw_url":"https:\/\/pastefy.app\/WNIPE6Vc\/raw","type":"PASTE","created_at":"2026-05-07 11:37:46.199","user":{"id":"jEzo2SQk","name":"Galactic","avatar":"https:\/\/accounts.interaapps.de\/avatars\/G.png","display_name":"Galactic"}},{"exists":true,"id":"vOmsPPrw","content":"Why Independent Pet Groomers and Dog Walkers Miss 35% of Their Booking Calls \u2014 and the $25\/Month Fix\n\nIf you run a solo dog grooming business or a dog walking service, you've probably had this experience: you're mid-groom, both hands occupied, phone buzzes on the counter. By the time you're done and can call back, the caller has already booked with someone else.\n\nIt's not a minor inconvenience. In markets like Denver, Nashville, and Raleigh where pet ownership rates are high and solo service providers are competing hard for regulars, a missed booking call is a missed $60 to $90 service appointment. Over a week, a month, a year, it adds up fast.\n\nIndustry surveys of solo pet care operators put the missed call rate at 30 to 40 percent during active service hours. That's the window when groomers are actually working on dogs and walkers are out on routes \u2014 exactly when calls come in. After-hours and early morning, the miss rate climbs higher because most solo operators don't answer outside their work window.\n\nThe math for a solo groomer running 20 appointments per week: if 15 to 20 inbound booking calls come in during that time and 35 percent go unanswered, that's 5 to 7 missed booking opportunities per week. At $70 average service value, that's $350 to $490 per week in potential revenue that never materializes \u2014 roughly $18,000 to $25,000 per year.\n\nTraditional solutions don't fit solo operators well.\n\nA full-time receptionist is out of the question at $2,200 to $3,200 per month before benefits. Live answering services like Ruby or Smith.ai start at $150 to $300 per month but can't actually book appointments in the calendar \u2014 they take a message and route it back, which still requires a return call that most callers won't wait for.\n\nThe shift in the past year has been the emergence of AI phone answering systems priced specifically for solo operators. The entry tier at $25 per month \u2014 roughly 100 minutes of call coverage \u2014 covers 30 to 50 inbound calls per month, which is the full inbound volume for most solo pet care businesses.\n\nThe system answers calls when the groomer is working, asks the caller for their preferred appointment day and time, checks the calendar, confirms availability, and books it directly \u2014 syncing to Google Calendar in real time. Callers get an SMS confirmation. The groomer gets a notification and sees the appointment already in the schedule.\n\nFor dog walkers, the same system handles the FAQ layer: routes, pricing, availability by neighborhood, recurring vs. one-time scheduling. Calls that are too complex \u2014 a specific behavioral situation the owner wants to discuss, a recurring client with a longstanding arrangement \u2014 route to a human.\n\nThe breakeven math is simple. At $25 per month, recovering one appointment per week that would otherwise have been missed pays for the service roughly eight times over.\n\nWhat it doesn't do: it won't have the personal conversation that a regular client expects from their longtime groomer. Solo pet care businesses where client relationships are the core competitive differentiator should think of this as overflow coverage \u2014 not a replacement for the personal touch that keeps regulars coming back.\n\nFor solo operators primarily dealing with new customer acquisition and booking calls, the volume problem is real and the cost to solve it has come down significantly.\n\nMore on the missed call cost data for solo service businesses: https:\/\/www.ringoperator.com\/blog\/missed-calls-cost-smbs-13kyear-2026-data-salons-clinics-trades\n","title":"Why Independent Pet Groomers and Dog Walkers Miss 35% of Their Booking Calls","encrypted":false,"visibility":"PUBLIC","raw_url":"https:\/\/pastefy.app\/vOmsPPrw\/raw","type":"PASTE","created_at":"2026-05-07 11:30:39.72"},{"exists":true,"id":"QAgY2bVd","content":"local CoreGui = game:GetService(\"CoreGui\")\nlocal RunService = game:GetService(\"RunService\")\nlocal TextService = game:GetService(\"TextService\")\nlocal StarterGui = game:GetService(\"StarterGui\")\nlocal TweenService = game:GetService(\"TweenService\")\n\nlocal ScreenGui = Instance.new(\"ScreenGui\")\nScreenGui.Parent = CoreGui\nScreenGui.Name = \"QuickConsole\"\nScreenGui.ZIndexBehavior = Enum.ZIndexBehavior.Sibling\nScreenGui.DisplayOrder = 5\nScreenGui.ResetOnSpawn = false\n\nlocal Button = Instance.new(\"TextButton\")\nButton.Parent = ScreenGui\nButton.Name = \"Button\"\nButton.BorderSizePixel = 0\nButton.BackgroundColor3 = Color3.fromRGB(0, 0, 0)\nButton.BackgroundTransparency = 0.15\nButton.Size = UDim2.new(0, 53, 0, 53)\nButton.Position = UDim2.new(0, 0, 0, 5)\nButton.Text = \"\"\nButton.Active = true\nButton.Draggable = true\nButton.AutoButtonColor = false\n\nlocal UICorner = Instance.new(\"UICorner\")\nUICorner.Parent = Button\nUICorner.CornerRadius = UDim.new(0, 15)\n\nlocal UIStroke = Instance.new(\"UIStroke\")\nUIStroke.Parent = Button\nUIStroke.ApplyStrokeMode = Enum.ApplyStrokeMode.Border\nUIStroke.Thickness = 1.2\nUIStroke.Color = Color3.fromRGB(255, 255, 255)\nUIStroke.Transparency = 0.85\n\nlocal Icon = Instance.new(\"ImageLabel\")\nIcon.Parent = Button\nIcon.Name = \"Icon\"\nIcon.BorderSizePixel = 0\nIcon.BackgroundTransparency = 1\nIcon.BackgroundColor3 = Color3.fromRGB(255, 255, 255)\nIcon.AnchorPoint = 